Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i grew up in northwest indiana  in a university town surrounded by corn  and soybean fields  and when i was in the fourth grade my  class went on a field trip to the art  institute of chicago  we were ready to see the water lilies  and american gothic  if you're at all familiar with the area  you know that in order to get there  we had to drive through gary indiana  gary was a classic company town founded  by u.s steel  and it was once considered a crown jewel  of the industrial world  called the city of the century people  flocked to this new town with high hopes  and the promise of good jobs  but when my classmates and i drove  through gary on the bus  we saw boarded up windows overgrown  grass  and i remember asking my seat mate where  are the people  over decades of consolidation  outsourcing and process  changes plant shutdown and gary and the  people who work their life too  when i think about gary i think about  the people who left  and the cat and mouse game they must  have had to play chasing jobs that  require their skills  i think about how that becomes harder  and harder to maintain  as jobs leave as skills become obsolete  and when there isn't a clear way to  develop your capabilities  where you are  in 2021 even without a global pandemic  millions of americans are still unable  to fully participate in the economy  and that's due in large part to a  mismatch between the skills that they  have  and the skills and greatest demand and  even further high costs for learning  and dominant paradigms about career  trajectories and about  how and when career learning should  happen  may pose a barrier to folks who might  otherwise invest in their capabilities  and all of that's a shame because  re-skilling at scale  has the ability to help our country  attack the profound  economic inequalities we face  it can also help us realize the full  potential of emerging industries  that need people to fuel their growth  and interestingly reskilling might even  help us recast the way we see ourselves  not as people with fixed abilities  one-trick ponies  but instead is people who are  fundamentally capable of growth  the good news is that we can decide to  develop infrastructure  to make reskilling at scale happen and  while there is absolutely a role for  government and for nonprofits  i'm going to focus on what companies can  do  companies need to be at the center of  re-skilling efforts because  they know their skill set needs and can  forecast those needs  better than anybody companies are also  in many ways  the direct beneficiary of any labor  force advancement  and conveniently companies have direct  access to much of the american workforce  by virtue of being employers so in order  to meet the moment for reskilling there  are  three things companies can do number one  come together to determine their skill  set needs  number two develop programming to teach  those skills  and number three play matchmaker between  learners and programming  and teach taken together  these steps can position our labor force  to skill up  and to participate more fully in the  economy  let's start with number one companies  coming together to determine the skills  that they need within their ranks  having a handle on skill set needs can  help companies refine the scope  of their reskilling efforts to ensure  that they're aligned with  their actual requirements it can also  help learners cut through the noise  and focus on what's actually important  to companies  so in that way having a handle on skill  set needs  can help players on both sides of the  equation align their efforts  a critical piece of this step is that  companies need to come together in order  to do this  and all suggest that they come together  at the industry level given that firms  within industries tend to have  important similarities all players in an  industry  have the ability to benefit when their  labor force is expanded and improved  so if everybody has the ability to  benefit everybody needs to be involved  our second step is for companies to  develop programming to teach the skill  sets that they need  and there are a few ways that they might  do this one approach  is to partner with organizations that  already teach the skills that they're  interested in  so depending on the skill set needs  depending on the target populations that  they're interested in engaging  there may already be a bevy of  non-profit and for-profit  players who provide offerings  colleges and universities may be an  obvious choice for certain companies  they have vast infrastructure for  learning and their bread and butter  is delivering credible learning  experiences at scale  on the other end companies may consider  developing programming from scratch  in certain circumstances they may find  that outside organizations may not be  well equipped  to teach the skills that they're  interested in having  they may also find that outside partners  may not serve the populations that  they're most interested in engaging  so for example many opportunities exist  for  people at the entry level to develop  their skills  but fewer opportunities exist for people  at later stages in their career  as such it may be appropriate for  companies to consider ways that they can  support learners  across their career span  our final step is for companies to play  matchmaker  between learners and programming and  actually implement these programs  and there are two sides of this on the  one end  companies can engage their existing  employees  acting as a facilitator they can help  their employees understand their  strengths  and their interests in the context of  the businesses needs  and then use that information to place  learners on the appropriate pathway  the result is that over time employees  and companies by extension  are able to modulate their capabilities  to meet the demands of the day  so instead of getting laid off employees  can find new purpose  and instead of expensive talent searches  companies can use the talent right in  front of them  the other side of this is companies  engaging perspective talent  you can imagine situations in which it  does make sense for a company to look  outside of its four walls particularly  if it's looking to expand its headcount  under this approach they can act as a  facilitator to help  prospective employees think about their  strengths and interests in the context  of business needs  and then use that information to connect  them to learning pathways  doing so allows companies to make sure  that they're filling all of their seats  and operating at peak capacity  if all of this sounds expensive to you  you're right  but so is recruiting every time you  can't find the skills that you need  in-house according to the society for  human resource management  it costs a u.s company an average of a  little over four  thousand dollars per employee to recruit  and that doesn't include the 40 to 50  days on average  it takes to find that employee  creating a re-skilling regime involves  reallocating the significant talent  investments  that companies are already making an  equally compelling cost though  comes from all of the opportunities not  seized  because companies don't have the  capabilities and capacity in house to  execute  and it's for these reasons that  re-skilling can't just be a pet  initiative  reskilling has the ability to unleash  the full potential of our workforce and  of our industries  and that's why companies from nike to  amazon to walmart  are all making these investments  now i want you to imagine for a moment  being called into your boss's office  you know that your division hasn't been  hitting its numbers despite  your hardest efforts and you also know  that the company would be silly not to  make changes  but when you sit down you're not met  with conversation about a pink slip  or talk about how you and the company  need to go in different directions  instead you're met with a conversation  about you  and the role that you can play in moving  the company forward  by growing your capabilities growing  your know-how  you can be the solution  when we make re-skilling the norm we can  make this situation the norm too  companies don't have to get up and leave  and workers don't have to be the cat  chasing the mouse we can decide to make  this our reality  thank you  you

